The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight, which is white light, consists of different colors with varying wavelengths. When sunlight passes through the Earth's atmosphere, shorter wavelengths of light (like blue and violet) are scattered more efficiently in all directions, while longer wavelengths (like red and yellow) are scattered less. This scattering makes blue light more visible, giving the sky its characteristic blue color. [end of text]


